The vulnerable construct is present in the `crypto_authenc_esn_genicv_tail` function (lines 54-68). This function manipulates high-order sequence number bits during out-of-place decryption. Specifically, lines 64-66 show the problematic logic:


scatterwalk_map_and_copy(tmp, dst, 4, 4, 0);
scatterwalk_map_and_copy(tmp + 1, dst, assoclen + cryptlen, 4, 0);
scatterwalk_map_and_copy(tmp, dst, 0, 8, 1);


These lines read from dst at offset 4 and assoclen+cryptlen, then write back to dst at offset 0. However, when src != dst (out-of-place operation), the dst buffer may not contain the correct sequence number bits at these offsets. The function should be reading from the source buffer (req->src) instead of the destination buffer (req->dst) to get the original sequence number bits before rearranging them. The same issue appears in `crypto_authenc_esn_genicv` (lines 119-122) and `crypto_authenc_esn_decrypt_tail` (lines 237-240), where sequence bits are being manipulated from dst instead of properly handling the source data. This causes data corruption by writing sequence bits to wrong offsets in dst when performing out-of-place operations.
